== How this document works

The prompt (`source-note.md`) poses the question in fourteen stages.
Each chapter answers one stage, in order.

Three rules follow from the prompt:

+ *Only look back.* A stage may use what earlier stages derived. It may not use later results. The "next step" part of each stage names the open question that the following stage takes up.
+ *No algorithm early.* P0 asks not to propose a final algorithm until asked. The first algorithm appears in Stage P11.
+ *Same six parts.* Each stage has: core view; derivation; when it holds; limits; alternatives; next step.

A thematic version of the same material exists as the project's `paper/`.
The derivations agree; this document differs in order and in what each stage is allowed to know.

== How claims are graded

P0 asks to separate rigorous results from approximations, empirical assumptions and conjectures.
Each claim carries one tag:

- #rig — proved under the model stated beside it.
- #cond — holds under extra assumptions, which the text names.
- #heur — a heuristic or conjecture.
- #open — not supported yet; needs a proof or an experiment.
- #wrong — an error, with the correction beside it.

P0 also warns against claiming two theories are equivalent because their formulas look alike.
Where two things share an equation, the text says whether they share a meaning.

== Provenance

The questions are the user's. The answers, derivations and algorithms are Claude's.
None was discussed with the user before writing. They are proposals.
Published methods are described as their authors define them; reinterpretations are marked as ours.

== Evidence used

There are no neural-network experiments.
Figures are closed-form curves, or Monte Carlo and finite-difference checks of closed forms, generated by `scripts/gen_figures.py`.
